# **TOPIC 4 — Redefining a Non-Virtual Function (Not Polymorphism!)**  
(CurvedActivity example in PDF) fileciteturn0file0

---

## **THEORY — IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE**

If a base class function is NOT virtual, the **actual function called depends on the variable type**, not the object type.

Example:

```cpp
Base *p = new Derived;
p->func();    // calls Base::func, NOT Derived::func (if not virtual)
```

This is called **static binding**.

---

## **CODE — CurvedActivity Redefining setScore()**

```cpp
// t4_redefine.cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class GradedActivity {
protected:
    double score;
public:
    void setScore(double s) { score = s; }
    double getScore() const { return score; }
};

class CurvedActivity : public GradedActivity {
    double percentage;
public:
    CurvedActivity(double p) : percentage(p) {}

    // REDEFINITION (not overriding)
    void setScore(double s) {
        double curved = s + (s * percentage);
        GradedActivity::setScore(curved);
    }
};

int main() {
    CurvedActivity c(0.10);  // +10% curve
    c.setScore(80);          // becomes 88
    cout << "Curved score = " << c.getScore() << "\n";
}
