# **TOPIC 5 — Virtual Functions & True Polymorphism**  
(PassFailActivity + displayGrade example, Program 15-10 to 15-11) fileciteturn0file0

---

## **THEORY — When You Need Virtual**

A **virtual function** ensures the function call depends on the **object type**, not pointer/reference type.

This is the basis of runtime polymorphism.

Rules:
- `virtual` in base class
- `override` (optional but recommended) in derived
- Always have a **virtual destructor**

---

## **CODE — Full Professional Polymorphism Example**

```cpp
// t5_virtual_polymorphism.cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class GradedActivity {
protected:
    double score;
public:
    GradedActivity(double s = 0) : score(s) {}

    virtual void setScore(double s) { score = s; }
    double getScore() const { return score; }

    virtual char getLetterGrade() const {
        if (score >= 90) return 'A';
        if (score >= 80) return 'B';
        if (score >= 70) return 'C';
        if (score >= 60) return 'D';
        return 'F';
    }

    virtual ~GradedActivity() {}    // IMPORTANT
};

class PassFailActivity : public GradedActivity {
    double minPassing;
public:
    PassFailActivity(double pass = 70)
        : GradedActivity(), minPassing(pass) {}

    void setScore(double s) override {
        score = s;
    }

    char getLetterGrade() const override {
        return (score >= minPassing ? 'P' : 'F');
    }
};

void display(const GradedActivity &g) {
    cout << "Score: " << g.getScore() << "\n";
    cout << "Grade: " << g.getLetterGrade() << "\n";
}

int main() {
    PassFailActivity p(70);
    p.setScore(72);

    display(p);    // Calls derived version because of "virtual"
}
```

**Expected Output**
```
Score: 72
Grade: P
```

