# ⭐ **TOPIC 6 — Abstract Classes (Pure Virtual Functions)**  
(This is where a class becomes “incomplete” and cannot be instantiated.)

---

# 🎯 **What is an abstract class?**

A class with **at least one pure virtual function**:

```cpp
virtual void func() = 0;
```

This means:

- The class cannot create objects.
- Derived classes **must** override this function.
- Used when base class defines a general concept.

Example concept:
```
Shape → Circle, Rectangle, Triangle
```

Shape should NOT be created directly.

---

# ⭐ **Ultra-simple example**

```cpp
class Shape {
public:
    virtual void draw() = 0;   // pure virtual
};
```

Derived:

```cpp
class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    void draw() override {
        cout << "Drawing circle\n";
    }
};
```

Test:

```cpp
int main() {
    Circle c;
    c.draw();
}
```

**Output**
```
Drawing circle
```

---

# ⭐ **KEY IDEA**

> Abstract class = “template class” with missing pieces.

Derived must “fill in” the missing functions.

---

---

# ⭐ **TOPIC 7 — Interfaces Using Abstract Classes**  
Your PDF explains that **C++ has no “interface” keyword**, so we use **abstract classes with only pure virtual functions**.

---

# 🎯 Interface in C++ looks like this:

```cpp
class Printable {
public:
    virtual void print() = 0;   // 100% abstract
};
```

Any class that wants to be “Printable” must implement:

```cpp
class Invoice : public Printable {
public:
    void print() override {
        cout << "Printing invoice...\n";
    }
};
```

