# ⭐ **TOPIC 7 — INTERFACES (Using Abstract Classes in C++)**

In C++, there is **no keyword “interface”** like Java.

So how do we make an interface?

👉 **We make a class that has ONLY pure virtual functions.**

That’s it.

---

# 🎯 **EASIEST POSSIBLE DEFINITION**

> An **interface** in C++ =  
> a class where **every function is = 0** (pure virtual),  
> and it has **no data**, **no normal functions**, **no constructor**.

This forces all child classes to **implement** the functions.

---

# ⭐ **ULTRA-SIMPLE REAL LIFE EXAMPLE**

Think of an interface as a **rulebook**.

Rulebook says:

```
To be Printable, you MUST have a print() function.
```

But rulebook itself **cannot print**.

---

# ⭐ **SIMPLE DIAGRAM**

```
Printable (interface)
   |
   +-- Invoice implements print()
   |
   +-- Report implements print()
   |
   +-- Card implements print()
```

All must follow the rule:

```
print()
```

---

# ⭐ **EASIEST POSSIBLE CODE**

### ✔ Interface (using abstract class)

```cpp
class Printable {
public:
    virtual void print() = 0;   // no body → pure virtual
};
```

### ✔ Class that uses (implements) the interface

```cpp
class Invoice : public Printable {
public:
    void print() override {
        cout << "Printing invoice\n";
    }
};
```

### ✔ Another class implementing interface

```cpp
class Card : public Printable {
public:
    void print() override {
        cout << "Printing greeting card\n";
    }
};
```

### ✔ Now use interface pointer

```cpp
void printAnything(Printable &p) {
    p.print();   // calls correct version (polymorphism)
}
```

### ✔ Full program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Printable {
public:
    virtual void print() = 0;  // interface function
};

class Invoice : public Printable {
public:
    void print() override {
        cout << "Printing invoice\n";
    }
};

class Card : public Printable {
public:
    void print() override {
        cout << "Printing greeting card\n";
    }
};

void printAnything(Printable &p) {
    p.print();         // polymorphic call
}

int main() {
    Invoice i;
    Card c;

    printAnything(i);  // "Printing invoice"
    printAnything(c);  // "Printing greeting card"
}
```

---

# ⭐ **OUTPUT**

```
Printing invoice
Printing greeting card
```

