# ⭐ **TOPIC 8 — Virtual Destructors (EASIEST EXPLANATION)**

---

# 🎯 **WHY WE NEED VIRTUAL DESTRUCTOR**

This problem ONLY happens when:

- You create a **Derived object**
- But use a **Base pointer** to delete it

Example:

```cpp
Base *p = new Derived;
delete p;
```

If Base's destructor is **NOT virtual**, the program will:

- Call Base destructor only ❌  
- Skip Derived destructor ❌  
- Memory may leak ❌  
- Resources may not be cleaned ❌  

---

# 🧠 **ULTRA-SIMPLE REAL LIFE STORY**

Imagine:

```
Base = Home
Derived = Home + Garage
```

If the demolition team ONLY knows “Home”, they will destroy only the Home part, not the Garage.

Garage remains → **leak**.

Virtual destructor tells demolition team:

> “Destroy FULL object, not just base part.”

---

# ⭐ **VERY EASY DIAGRAM**

Without virtual:

```
delete p
  |
  --> Base destructor runs only ❌
```

With virtual:

```
delete p
  |
  --> Derived destructor runs ✔
  --> Base destructor runs ✔
```

Correct order.

---

# ⭐ **ULTRA-SIMPLE CODE (BEGINNER VERSION)**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Base {
public:
    virtual ~Base() {    // VIRTUAL destructor
        cout << "Base destroyed\n";
    }
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
    ~Derived() {
        cout << "Derived destroyed\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Base *p = new Derived;
    delete p;
}
```

---

# ⭐ **OUTPUT**

```
Derived destroyed
Base destroyed
```

This means:

1. Derived destructor ran ✔  
2. Base destructor ran ✔  
3. No memory leak ✔  

---

# ⭐ **What happens if destructor is NOT virtual?**  
(Let me show you a short code to prove the problem)

```cpp
class Base {
public:
    ~Base() { cout << "Base destroyed\n"; }
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
    ~Derived() { cout << "Derived destroyed\n"; }
};
```

```cpp
Base* p = new Derived;
delete p;
```

**OUTPUT**
```
Base destroyed
```

Derived destructor does NOT run → **BAD**.

---

# ⭐ EASY RULE TO REMEMBER

> **If your class has ANY virtual function →  
Make destructor virtual too.**

This prevents hidden bugs.

